  "body": "From the algorithm of [hash attribute's setter](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#dom-location-hash), it seems to me if you do `location.hash = \"\"`, it should be identical to `location.hash = \"#\"`, which sets the fragment to empty string, and eventually invokes [scroll to the fragment](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#scroll-to-the-fragment-identifier) which scrolls the page to the top.\r\n\r\nHowever, this doesn't match browsers' behavior. At least, Firefox, Chrome, and Safari (I haven't tested Edge) all agree on that, when you call `location.hash = \"\"`, the document would be loaded again, while setting that to `\"#\"` doesn't trigger that.",
